 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Your city street is brand new, smooth asphalt from curb to curb.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then the water company needs a pipe, so they dig it up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "They patch it. Six months later, the sewer crew digs it up again.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then storm drains. Then power cables. Then internet.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Every patch is weaker than the road around it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Rain gets into the seams, and potholes open along every trench.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And nobody knows exactly where the old pipes are.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "One day, a crew digs right through the water main.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you draw a map, and give every pipe its own lane and its own depth.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Water on one side, sewer on the other, storm drain in the middle.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "You add shafts with round lids, so crews can reach the pipes without digging.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And valves, so one repair shuts off one block, not the whole street.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Finally, a rule. Every company digs in the same week, before the new asphalt goes down.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then nobody touches that street for five years.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just stopped your street from being dug up. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
